/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Run a closed traverse from A through B, C, D and back toward A.
   - e is the closing error in metres; ΣL is the algebraic sum of latitudes in metres, north positive and south negative; ΣD is the algebraic sum of departures in metres, east positive and west negative.
   - θ is the acute angle from the north–south line.
   - With ΣL = −0.23 m and ΣD = −0.12 m, tan θ = 0.12/0.23 = 0.522, so θ = 27°33′.
   - Size alone is not enough.
   - Two different misclosures exist.
   - Correct large angular misclosure first.
   - The Bowditch rule, also called the compass rule, spreads the linear misclosure in proportion to line length.
   - CLi is the correction to latitude in metres; CDi is the correction to departure in metres; ΣL and ΣD are the misclosures in metres; lᵢ is the length of line i in metres; P is the perimeter in metres.
   - The minus sign is essential: corrections act opposite to the misclosure.
*/
