/* book Worked example: Q: The closed compass traverse ABCD of the latitude and departure chapter has AB = 200 m at 45°, BC = 180 m at 135°, CD = 160 m at 210° and DA = 226 m at 303°20′, so ΣL = −0.23 m and ΣD = −0.12 m. Find the closing error, its bearing and the relative precision. Adjust the traverse by the Bowditch rule and give the coordinates of A, B, C and D, taking A as (1000.00 E, 1000.00 N).
   1. Closing error: e = √((−0.23)² + (−0.12)²) = √(0.0529 + 0.0144) = √0.0673 = 0.259 m.
   2. Bearing: tan θ = 0.12/0.23 = 0.522, θ = 27°33′. Both sums are negative, so A′ is south-west of A: bearing 180° + 27°33′ = 207°33′.
   3. Perimeter P = 200 + 180 + 160 + 226 = 766 m. Relative precision = 766/0.259 = 2953, so about 1 in 2950.
   4. Latitude corrections CL = +0.23 × l/766: AB +0.060, BC +0.054, CD +0.048, DA +0.068 m; sum +0.230 m.
   5. Departure corrections CD = +0.12 × l/766: AB +0.031, BC +0.028, CD +0.025, DA +0.036 m (the last rounded up 1 mm so the sum is exactly +0.120 m).
   6. Adjusted latitudes: AB +141.480, BC −127.226, CD −138.512, DA +124.258 m (sum 0.000). Adjusted departures: AB +141.451, BC +127.308, CD −79.975, DA −188.784 m (sum 0.000).
   7. Coordinates: B = (1000.000 + 141.451, 1000.000 + 141.480) = (1141.451, 1141.480); C = (1268.759, 1014.254); D = (1188.784, 875.742). Adding DA gives (1000.000, 1000.000), back at A.
   Answer: Closing error 0.259 m at 207°33′, relative precision about 1 in 2950; adjusted coordinates A (1000.00, 1000.00), B (1141.45, 1141.48), C (1268.76, 1014.25), D (1188.78, 875.74), in metres east and north. */
