/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Let every corner have coordinates (E, N): E is easting, the distance east of the origin, and N is northing, the distance north.
   - A is the area in square metres; Ei is the easting of corner i; Ni+1 and Ni−1 are the northings of the next and previous corners; the sum runs once around all n corners.
   - The same area has a second form.
   - Ei, Ni are the coordinates of corner i; Ei+1, Ni+1 are those of the next corner; the last pair uses corner 1 after corner n.
   - Both formulas return twice the area, not the area.
   - Take the corners in one direction around the boundary, clockwise or anticlockwise.
   - The sign of the sum fixes the direction.
   - Keep both coordinates in the same unit before multiplying.
   - Double meridian distance (DMD) is the older name for the same job done from latitudes and departures.
   - The coordinates may come from a closed traverse after adjustment, from a total station, or from a GNSS receiver.
*/
